<div class="case-study">
<div class="case-heading"><span class="eyebrow">UI/UX case study · Project 2</span><h1>Catalyst,<br/><em>reimagined.</em></h1><p>A student portal built around the next step — from finding a class to knowing you’re enrolled.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href="#/classes">Explore the prototype<Icon name="arrow" size={16}/></a><a class="button secondary" href={project.figma} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View Figma<Icon name="external" size={16}/></a></div><div class="case-meta"><div><span>Team</span><strong>{project.team.join(' · ')}</strong></div><div><span>Focus</span><strong>Class discovery, enrollment & academic clarity</strong></div><div><span>Stack</span><strong>Svelte 5 · JavaScript · Vite</strong></div></div></div>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">01 / The challenge</span><h2>Important tasks shouldn’t feel like a maze.</h2><p>Catalyst supports academic and administrative work at the University of Cincinnati. The team’s critique focused on class search and enrollment: students need to find a suitable section, understand their status, and recover when something prevents registration.</p><p>The redesign targets usability and information architecture (category A in the assignment). It changes how related tasks connect and how planning transitions into confirmed enrollment, alongside a calmer visual hierarchy.</p><figure class="case-image"><img src="./research/original-home.png" alt="Original Catalyst homepage: translucent category tiles displayed over a detailed nighttime campus photograph." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Original interface screenshot supplied in the team’s critique presentation. It illustrates the visual baseline; reported interaction failures require separate observation.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">02 / Research & evidence</span><h2>Make the reasoning visible.</h2><div class="notice"><Icon name="info"/><p><strong>Research status</strong><br/>Completed for this implementation: background research and a heuristic evidence review. No new student interviews, think-aloud sessions, or measured before/after study are claimed. Severity ratings below are design-review judgments, not participant measurements.</p></div><h3>Method 1 · Background research</h3><p>Reviewed the three sources below and mapped their guidance to specific controls and interaction states. These are design principles, not proof of improved user performance.</p><div class="research-cards">{#each research as r}<article class="panel padded"><a class="text-link" href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{r.title}<Icon name="external" size={15}/></a><p>{r.finding}</p><strong class="small">Applied in the prototype</strong><p class="muted small">{r.application}</p></article>{/each}</div><h3>Method 2 · Heuristic evidence review</h3><p>Reviewed the supplied screenshot, critique decks, script, and initial prototype source against Nielsen’s principles. Separated directly visible/source-verifiable issues from class-reported behavior. Scope: homepage orientation, course discovery, registration status, and error recovery. Severity: 0 no issue; 1 cosmetic; 2 moderate; 3 major; 4 task-blocking.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption class="sr-only">Heuristic findings, evidence, and resulting redesign decisions</caption><thead><tr><th>Finding & evidence</th><th>Principle / severity</th><th>Design response</th></tr></thead><tbody>{#each findings as f}<tr><td><strong>{f.id} · {f.problem}</strong><p class="muted small">{f.evidence}</p></td><td>{f.principle}<p class="small muted">{f.severity}</p></td><td>{f.decision}</td></tr>{/each}</tbody></table></div><p class="fine-print">Review date: {project.researchDate}. This review covers the supplied project artifacts. It is not a multi-evaluator expert study; additional participant research remains outstanding.</p></section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">03 / Design exploration</span><h2>One workspace. A visible next step.</h2><p>The supplied Figma explores five desktop frames: home list view, home calendar view, classes & schedule, academic progress, and academic services. The implementation uses college-inspired red and black with neutral surfaces while extending the design to cover interaction states absent from the static frames.</p><div class="design-principles"><article><span>01</span><h3>Bring related actions together</h3><p>Search, inspect, compare, cart, and confirmed schedule share one Classes & schedule workspace.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Say what the state means</h3><p>“In cart” is planning. “Waitlisted” is waiting. Only a successful confirmation means enrolled.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Make recovery part of the task</h3><p>Explain the exact conflict or missing prerequisite, then point toward a practical next action.</p></article></div><div class="case-palette"><span style:background="#a0010f">College red</span><span style:background="#111111">Black</span><span style:background="#e00122">Bright red</span><span style:background="#f6f6f6" style:color="#202020">White & gray</span></div><a class="text-link" href={project.figma} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the original Figma frames<Icon name="external" size={16}/></a></section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">04 / The working interface</span><h2>From “maybe this class” to “you’re all set.”</h2><div class="feature-cards"><article class="panel padded"><Icon name="search" size={26}/><h3>Find & compare</h3><p>Search by code, title, or instructor. Filter by subject, meeting day, format, availability, and conflicts. Compare up to three sections without losing search context.</p><a class="text-link" href="#/classes">Try class search<Icon name="arrow" size={15}/></a></article><article class="panel padded"><Icon name="shield" size={26}/><h3>Review & recover</h3><p>Term-specific carts preserve choices. Enrollment checks prerequisites, seats, duplicates, overlapping meetings, and the credit limit. Problems explain how to continue.</p><a class="text-link" href="#/classes">Explore enrollment<Icon name="arrow" size={15}/></a></article><article class="panel padded"><Icon name="check" size={26}/><h3>Confirm & keep track</h3><p>A receipt records successful enrollment. The schedule updates immediately, with list/week views, calendar export, and a mobile agenda. Drops include confirmation and immediate undo.</p></article><article class="panel padded"><Icon name="chart" size={26}/><h3>Understand progress</h3><p>Earned, in-progress, planned, and remaining coursework stay distinct. Review a synthetic transcript, graduation checklist, or readable downloadable summary.</p><a class="text-link" href="#/progress">View academic progress<Icon name="arrow" size={15}/></a></article></div>{#if project.screenshots.length}<div class="case-gallery">{#each project.screenshots as shot}<figure><img src={shot.src} alt={shot.alt} loading="lazy"/><figcaption>{shot.caption}</figcaption></figure>{/each}</div>{/if}</section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">05 / Feedback & evaluation</span><h2>What changed — and what still needs people.</h2><p>The implementation responds to the source review: planned courses no longer populate enrollment; carts belong to a term; mobile schedules retain their content; and enrollment has validation plus a confirmation. These are implementation changes, not reported participant feedback.</p><div class="notice warning-notice"><Icon name="people"/><p><strong>Participant feedback has not been collected.</strong><br/>The repository includes a ready-to-run think-aloud protocol, task script, recording sheet, and feedback log. Run it with real students, then add anonymized observations and resulting revisions here. Do not present a prepared protocol as a completed study.</p></div><p>Suggested tasks: compare two sections of CS5167; add CS5002 section 001 and CS5167 section 001 and resolve their overlap; complete enrollment; explain whether a cart or waitlist reserves a seat; find the same schedule on a narrow screen.</p><p>Record completion, errors, wrong turns, explanation of enrollment status, and participant suggestions. Compare the same tasks on the original interface only when participants have appropriate access. Keep prototype test results separate from measurements of actual Catalyst.</p></section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">06 / Implementation</span><h2>A focused front end, with meaningful behavior.</h2><p>Svelte 5 handles the component interface. Vite produces a portable static build; hash-based navigation keeps links working on static hosts. The original repository’s sample plans and transcripts are retained. A pure JavaScript domain module handles enrollment validation and is tested separately from the interface.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>src/App.svelte</td><td>Responsive shell, routes, command search, demo profiles, theme</td></tr><tr><td>src/pages/</td><td>Home, classes, academic progress, services, account pages, this case study</td></tr><tr><td>src/lib/model.js</td><td>Validation, carts, enrollment transactions, drops, progress, calendar export</td></tr><tr><td>src/lib/catalog.js</td><td>Synthetic profiles, sample sections, terms, and service links</td></tr><tr><td>src/lib/store.js</td><td>Per-profile local persistence, safe hydration, preferences, downloads</td></tr><tr><td>tests/</td><td>Behavioral checks for conflicts, term boundaries, eligibility, persistence, and credits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>There is no real authentication, backend, university connection, live seat inventory, financial account, notification service, or payment flow. All student records and registration actions are synthetic; only external university links leave the prototype. Browser storage failures display a notice.</p><a class="button secondary" href={project.repository} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><Icon name="code" size={17}/>Original project repository<Icon name="external" size={15}/></a><p class="fine-print">The linked upstream repository remains the original until the implementation branch is pushed or merged.</p></section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">Presentation</span><h2>Walk through the design story.</h2><p>The eight-slide final presentation includes the problem, research, core workflow, implementation, verification, and next steps. Speaker notes give each team member a timed section.</p><a class="button secondary" href="./presentation.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open final presentation<Icon name="external" size={15}/></a></section>
<section class="case-section" id="demo"><span class="eyebrow">07 / Demo</span><h2>See the workflow in action.</h2>{#if project.demoVideo}<p class="muted">Guided walkthrough with synthetic narration over captured prototype states from the earlier theme and sample profile. All student records are fictional; this is not a participant test or a live screen recording.</p><video controls preload="metadata"><source src={project.demoVideo} type="video/mp4"/><track kind="captions" src="./demo-captions.vtt" srclang="en" label="English" default/></video>{:else}<div class="demo-placeholder panel padded"><span class="tile-icon"><Icon name="play" size={28}/></span><div><h3>Guided demo & recording script</h3><p>The recording script in docs/demo-script.md is ready. Add the completed 2–3 minute recording to publish it here.</p></div><a href="#/classes" class="button primary">Open the live prototype<Icon name="arrow" size={16}/></a></div>{/if}</section>
<section class="case-section"><span class="eyebrow">08 / Future work</span><h2>The next iteration starts with listening.</h2><ol class="future-list"><li><strong>Collect student feedback.</strong> Run the prepared tasks, document observed issues, and revise the design from evidence.</li><li><strong>Validate accessibility.</strong> Add screen-reader sessions and broader zoom, keyboard, and contrast evaluation; no WCAG conformance claim is made.</li><li><strong>Extend registration realism.</strong> Model linked lecture/lab sections, conditional prerequisites, term-specific deadlines, and live seat changes only if needed.</li><li><strong>Confirm academic rules.</strong> Replace illustrative classifications and graduation assumptions with an advisor-approved requirement model.</li></ol><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href="#/">Return to the student workspace<Icon name="arrow" size={16}/></a><button class="button secondary" onclick={()=>window.print()}><Icon name="file" size={16}/>Print case study</button></div></section>
